# POML VS Code Extension — Complete Developer Reference

## Quick Reference

### Build & Run Locally
```bash
# 1. Build all platform binaries
bash scripts/build-binaries.sh

# 2. Install extension dependencies
cd vscode-extension && npm install

# 3. Start TypeScript watcher (in one terminal)
npm run esbuild-watch

# 4. Debug in VS Code (in another terminal)
code . && # Press F5 to launch extension
```

### Commands
```bash
npm run esbuild           # Build TypeScript once
npm run esbuild-watch    # Watch for changes and rebuild
npm run lint             # Check for linting issues
npm test                 # Run unit tests
npm run vscode:prepublish # Production build (minified)
npm run package          # Create .vsix package
npm run publish          # Publish to VS Code Marketplace
```

### File Manifest

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `src/extension.ts` | VS Code extension entrypoint; handles activation, commands, status bar |
| `src/manager.ts` | Binary detection, spawning, lifecycle management |
| `src/server.ts` | MCP server adapter; proxies MCP calls to HTTP endpoints |
| `src/http-client.ts` | HTTP client wrapper for poml API |
| `scripts/build-binaries.sh` | Cross-compiles poml for all platforms |
| `.github/workflows/build-release.yml` | Automated CI/CD pipeline for building and publishing |
| `package.json` | Extension manifest, dependencies, scripts |
| `tsconfig.json` | TypeScript configuration |
| `jest.config.js` | Testing framework configuration |
| `.eslintrc.js` | Code linting rules |
| `.vscode/launch.json` | Debug configuration (F5) |
| `README.md` | Extension documentation for users |
| `VSCODE_EXTENSION_SUMMARY.md` | Implementation overview |
| `RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md` | Pre-release and publishing checklist |

---

## Architecture

```
MCP Client (Claude Desktop, Cline, etc.)
         ↓
    stdin/stdout (MCP Protocol)
         ↓
   Extension (src/server.ts)
         ↓
    HTTP Client (src/http-client.ts)
         ↓
   localhost:7777 (HTTP)
         ↓
   poml binary (src/manager.ts spawned)
         ↓
   .poml file (active editor)
```

---

## Environment Setup

### Development
- Go 1.25+ (for building binaries)
- Node.js 18+ (for extension)
- VS Code 1.80+ (for testing)
- Git (for version control)

### CI/CD
- GitHub Actions (free for public repos)
- PAT tokens for marketplace publishing (optional; manual publishing also works)

---

## Testing Strategy

### Unit Tests
```bash
npm test                          # Run all tests
npm test -- manager.test.ts       # Run specific test file
npm test -- --coverage            # Generate coverage report
```

**Coverage Targets:**
- Lines: 50%+
- Functions: 50%+
- Branches: 50%+
- Statements: 50%+

### Integration Tests
```bash
npm test integration.test.ts      # Full workflow tests
```

**What to Test:**
1. Binary detection and execution
2. Server startup/shutdown
3. MCP tool invocation (all 6 tools)
4. Error handling and fallbacks
5. Cross-platform binary compatibility

### Manual Testing
1. Open `.poml` file in VS Code
2. Server should auto-start
3. Run MCP tool commands via client
4. Verify results match poml CLI output

---

## Publishing Workflow

### 1. Pre-Release
```bash
# Update version in package.json
# Update CHANGELOG.md
# Commit and push
git add .
git commit -m "chore: release v0.1.0"
git push origin main
```

### 2. Create Tag
```bash
git tag v0.1.0
git push origin --tags
```

### 3. GitHub Actions Automates
- Builds all 6 binaries
- Packages extension
- Creates GitHub Release
- Publishes to marketplaces (if tokens configured)

### 4. Verify
- Check GitHub Release page
- Wait for marketplace sync (~1–24 hours for VS Code, ~1 hour for Open VSX)
- Test install from marketplace

---

## Marketplace Accounts

### VS Code Marketplace
- **URL:** https://marketplace.visualstudio.com
- **Publisher:** Create at https://dev.azure.com/_usersSettings/tokens
- **Token Scope:** Marketplace (Manage)
- **Cost:** Free
- **Setup:** Set `VSCODE_MARKETPLACE_TOKEN` secret in GitHub

### Open VSX Registry
- **URL:** https://open-vsx.org
- **Account:** Create at https://open-vsx.org/user/profile
- **Token Scope:** Publishing
- **Cost:** Free
- **Setup:** Set `OVSX_TOKEN` secret in GitHub

### MCP Registry
- **URL:** https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io
- **Type:** Community registry
- **Cost:** Free
- **Process:** Manual registration after marketplace publication

---

## Binary Distribution Details

### Platforms Supported
| OS | Arch | Binary Name | Notes |
|----|----|---|---|
| macOS | amd64 | `poml` | Intel Macs |
| macOS | arm64 | `poml` | M1/M2/M3 Macs |
| Linux | amd64 | `poml` | Intel Linux |
| Linux | arm64 | `poml` | ARM Linux (Raspberry Pi, etc.) |
| Windows | amd64 | `poml.exe` | Intel Windows |
| Windows | arm64 | `poml.exe` | ARM Windows |

### Binary Detection Logic
1. Detect platform (`process.platform`) and arch (`process.arch`)
2. Map Node.js arch (`x64`) to Go arch (`amd64`)
3. Look for bundled binary at `bin/{platform}/{arch}/poml[.exe]`
4. If not found, fall back to system PATH
5. If neither found, show error with installation instructions

### Size Optimization
- Build flags: `-ldflags="-s -w"` (strip symbols and debug info)
- Result: ~15 MB per platform
- Total bundled: ~100 MB (6 platforms)
- Extracted (users): ~15 MB per platform

---

## MCP Tools Reference

| Tool | Input | Output | Use Case |
|------|-------|--------|----------|
| `poml_validate` | None | Validation result | Check POML syntax/semantics |
| `poml_convert` | format (json/dict/openai_chat/langchain) | Converted doc | Export to other formats |
| `poml_search` | query, limit | Search results | Find tags/attributes/text |
| `poml_patch` | path, value | Updated doc | Mutate elements |
| `poml_diagram` | format (mermaid/json) | Diagram | Visualize structure |
| `poml_get_ast` | None | Full AST | Access document tree |

---

## Troubleshooting

### Extension Won't Start
- Check if `.poml` file is open
- Verify binary exists: `ls -la vscode-extension/bin/`
- Check VS Code output: View → Output → POML MCP

### Port Already in Use
- Change port in `manager.ts` constructor
- Or kill process: `lsof -i :7777 | grep LISTEN | awk '{print $2}' | xargs kill -9`

### Binary Not Found
- Run `bash scripts/build-binaries.sh`
- Check `echo $GOOS $GOARCH` for current platform
- Fall back to system: `go install github.com/atlas-foundry/poml-go-sdk/cmd/poml@latest`

### Tests Failing
- Delete `node_modules/`: `rm -rf node_modules`
- Reinstall: `npm install`
- Rebuild binaries: `bash scripts/build-binaries.sh`

### TypeScript Errors
- Ensure binaries exist (see above)
- Run `npm run lint` to check code style
- Use VS Code TypeScript language server (built-in)

---

## Performance Considerations

### Binary Size
- Extension size in marketplace: ~2 MB (code only)
- Downloaded size: ~15 MB per platform (binaries)
- Installation time: ~30 seconds (network-dependent)

### Memory Usage
- VS Code extension: ~50–100 MB
- poml HTTP server: ~20–50 MB (depends on file size)
- Total: ~100–150 MB

### Startup Time
- Extension activation: &lt;1 second
- Server startup: 1–2 seconds
- First MCP call: ~100–500 ms (includes HTTP roundtrip)

### Caching
- Extension caches binary path after first detection
- HTTP client reuses connection pool
- Server keeps file in memory (fast access)

---

## Contributing Guidelines

### Code Style
- Follow TypeScript strict mode
- Use ESLint: `npm run lint`
- Format with Prettier: See `.prettierrc`
- Commit messages: Use Conventional Commits (feat:, fix:, chore:, etc.)

### Testing
- Write tests for new features
- Run `npm test` before pushing
- Aim for 50%+ coverage (target 80%+)

### Documentation
- Update `README.md` for user-facing changes
- Update this guide for developer-facing changes
- Use POML format for new docs (except this reference)

---

## Release Cycle

### Patch (0.0.1 → 0.0.2)
- Bug fixes only
- Released ASAP (same day if critical)
- No feature additions

### Minor (0.1.0 → 0.2.0)
- New features
- Released every 2–4 weeks
- Backward compatible

### Major (1.0.0 → 2.0.0)
- Breaking changes
- Coordinate with Go SDK major release
- Documented migration guide

